an. Peltola has out-raised Sullivan substantially, though he holds more cash on hand and benefits from incumbency in a state with a six-point Republican lean. Recent polling is mixed: independent surveys, including a New York Times/Siena poll, show Peltola ahead by several points, while Republican-affiliated pollsters show the race tied or Sullivan slightly up.\n\nMost expert raters call the race a tossup, with a couple rating it a narrow Democratic edge, and prediction markets price Peltola around 70%. High gas prices and voter frustration appear to be working in her favor, though a recent news report on Peltola could complicate things. The race remains genuinely competitive, with Peltola holding a modest edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5747, "margin": 0.87}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6955, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6995, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6965, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6035, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5728, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6289, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.6246, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.603, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.6381, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.6392, "margin": 1.5}]}